3. Split the plugin into an entry point and a pass
================================================

Task: create the pass interface, its implementation, and its registration:

  include/minishield/FlatteningPass.h — class and run() declaration
  lib/FlatteningPass.cpp             — run() definition
  lib/Plugin.cpp                     — plugin entry point and registration

The header lets other translation units know the pass class exists. Each
.cpp file is compiled separately, so Plugin.cpp needs to include that header.
CMake must also list the implementation .cpp file to compile and link it.

For a function pass, run() receives:

  llvm::Function &F
  llvm::FunctionAnalysisManager &AM

It returns llvm::PreservedAnalyses. The initial implementation only reports
the function name and block count, so it returns PreservedAnalyses::all().

Observed mistakes: the header was initially named FlatteningPass.cpp, while
the includes requested FlatteningPass.h. Rename the declaration file to .h.
Plugin.cpp also needed semicolons after the callback registration expression
and the returned aggregate initializer. Read the compiler location and check
the surrounding statement boundaries when diagnosing these syntax errors.


4. Choose optional or required pass behavior explicitly
======================================================

Task: compare PassInfoMixin, OptionalPassInfoMixin, and RequiredPassInfoMixin
in the installed llvm/IR/PassManager.h.

PassInfoMixin supplies shared metadata such as the pass name and pipeline
printing. In this installation it retains optional behavior for compatibility.
The header recommends inheriting from an explicit optional or required mixin.

  OptionalPassInfoMixin<T>: isRequired() is false; the pass may be skipped.
  RequiredPassInfoMixin<T>: isRequired() is true; normal pass-skipping
                            mechanisms must not skip the scheduled pass.

For example, an optional function pass may be skipped on an optnone function.
A required pass still runs there. Required does not automatically schedule
the pass: pipeline registration and invocation are still necessary.

We used OptionalPassInfoMixin for the flattening stub. We chose
RequiredPassInfoMixin for statistics because inspection should also report
functions marked optnone.


5. Register the pass name that opt will recognize
================================================

Task: understand llvmGetPassPluginInfo() in Plugin.cpp.

The exported entry point returns PassPluginLibraryInfo containing the LLVM
plugin API version, the plugin name, the plugin version, and a callback that
registers passes with PassBuilder.

Use llvm/Plugins/PassPlugin.h in this installed LLVM version. Return the
header-provided LLVM_PLUGIN_API_VERSION; no custom plugin-version macro is
needed. The plugin's own version string is distinct from that API version.

The installed function-pipeline parsing callback takes:

  llvm::StringRef Name
  llvm::FunctionPassManager &FPM
  llvm::ArrayRef<llvm::PassBuilder::PipelineElement> InnerPipeline

It recognizes a name, adds the corresponding pass to FPM, and returns true.
It returns false for a name it does not handle. Our simple passes also reject
unexpected nested pipelines by checking InnerPipeline.

Checkpoint: trace how the string minishield-flatten results in constructing
FlatteningPass and adding it to the function pass manager.


