15. Declare a reusable CFGAnalyzer analysis
=========================================

Task: write include/minishield/CFGAnalyzer.h. This header is now drafted.
Keep #pragma once and remember the semicolon after the class's closing brace.

Include llvm/IR/PassManager.h, llvm/IR/BasicBlock.h, and <vector>.
Inside namespace minishield, inherit publicly from:

  llvm::AnalysisInfoMixin<CFGAnalyzer>

Define public BlockInfo and Result structures:

  struct BlockInfo {
      const llvm::BasicBlock *block;
      std::vector<const llvm::BasicBlock *> successors;
      std::vector<const llvm::BasicBlock *> predecessors;
  };

  struct Result {
      std::vector<BlockInfo> blocks;
  };

Declare this public method:

  Result run(
      llvm::Function &F,
      llvm::FunctionAnalysisManager &AM);

Add these private members:

  friend llvm::AnalysisInfoMixin<CFGAnalyzer>;
  static llvm::AnalysisKey Key;

A printing or transformation pass returns PreservedAnalyses. An analysis
returns computed data, its Result. LLVM's analysis manager can cache that
result and make it available to other passes.

The analysis key provides a unique identity. The friend declaration lets
AnalysisInfoMixin access that private key. Declaring the static member in
the header is separate from defining it in the future implementation file.

The block pointers refer to objects owned by the function. Result neither
copies nor owns the blocks. If transformations change the IR, consumers
must respect analysis invalidation rather than continue using stale data.

Checkpoint: explain how returning BlockInfo objects will let a future pass
use the graph without parsing text printed by StatsPass.


