17. Understand references, pointers, and addresses while implementing the CFG
===========================================================================

The learner drafted CFGAnalyzer.cpp and assigned BB directly to info.block.
The review identified a type mismatch: BB is a reference to a block, while
info.block holds a pointer to a block.

Start by reading the declaration in the block loop:

  for (const llvm::BasicBlock &BB : F)

The & in this declaration makes BB a reference. A reference is another name
for an existing object; it is not a pointer variable. Use it like the object:

  BB.getName()

Compare that with the member declaration:

  const llvm::BasicBlock *block;

The * declares a pointer, which stores an object's address. To assign the
address of the block that BB refers to, write:

  info.block = &BB;

Here & is an operator in an expression: it obtains the block's address.
It does not copy the block or create a separate reference object. A compiler
may implement references using addresses internally, but that does not make
a reference interchangeable with a pointer in C++ source code.

Read each form according to its context:

  const llvm::BasicBlock &BB    Declare a reference to a block.
  &BB                          Obtain the referenced block's address.
  const llvm::BasicBlock *ptr   Declare a pointer to a block.
  *ptr                         Access the object that ptr points to.
  BB.getName()                 Access a member through a reference.
  ptr->getName()               Access a member through a pointer.

Now compare the successor loop:

  for (const llvm::BasicBlock *successor : llvm::successors(&BB)) {
      info.successors.push_back(successor);
  }

successor is already a pointer because its declaration uses *. Append it
directly. Taking &successor would obtain the address of the local pointer
variable, not the address of the block. That would add another pointer level
and would not match the vector's element type.

Apply the same reasoning to predecessors. These pointers do not own the
blocks; the function owns them. They must not be used after the referenced
blocks have been deleted or the analysis result has become invalid.

The implementation review also supplied three related corrections:

  Use Result result{}; rather than Result result = new Result();.
  new returns a pointer, while run() returns a Result value.

  Use llvm::BasicBlock with two colons for namespace qualification.

  Use llvm::successors() and llvm::predecessors(), both plural.

Checkpoint: explain why info.block needs &BB, while push_back(successor)
needs no additional &. Then fix the implementation yourself, add its source
to CMake, and build before registering the analysis with LLVM.

18. Read pointer and reference syntax in traversal loops
======================================================

The learner asked why some traversals use * and others use &. Start with
the type of each range's elements, rather than memorizing one loop pattern.

  for (const llvm::BasicBlock &BB : F)

Iterating a function gives access to its basic block objects by reference.
BB is a reference to each existing block. It does not copy the block. Use
BB.getName() to access members. The instruction loop follows the same rule:

  for (const llvm::Instruction &I : BB)

Compare the successor traversal:

  for (const llvm::BasicBlock *successor : llvm::successors(&BB))

The successors range yields block pointers, so successor is a pointer. The
loop copies an address, not a BasicBlock. Use successor->getName(). The &BB
inside the function call obtains the block's address because this overload
of successors() takes a pointer. That expression is distinct from the & in
the declaration of the outer loop's reference variable.

Now examine the analysis result, whose types you defined yourself:

  for (const auto &info : cfg.blocks)

cfg.blocks is a vector of BlockInfo records. const auto &info refers to
each stored record. Without &, auto info would copy the record and its
successor/predecessor vectors on every iteration. Avoid those unnecessary
copies when only inspecting the records.

  for (const auto *successor : info.successors)

info.successors is a vector of pointers to const blocks. Each element is
already a pointer, so copying it is appropriate. The block itself is not
copied. The predecessor loops work the same way.

Likewise, in:

  const auto &cfg = AM.getResult<CFGAnalyzer>(F);

the reference lets StatsPass inspect the cached result without copying its
records and vectors. The analysis manager owns that cached result.

Checkpoint: annotate these four operations in your own words:

  BB.getName()          Access through an object/reference.
  &BB                   Obtain that object's address.
  successor->getName()  Access through a pointer.
  *successor            Access the object identified by the pointer.

The rule is to follow the element type: references for existing records or
objects when avoiding copies, pointer variables when the elements already
hold addresses. There is no rule that every traversal needs * or every
traversal needs &. Keep const when the traversal is read-only.

19. Understand why auto alone tries to copy a basic block
=======================================================

During the function eligibility exercise, the learner asked why this loop
cannot replace the explicit reference declaration:

  for (auto bb : F)

Teach the difference between type deduction and reference binding. auto
deduces the type, but a plain auto variable does not automatically preserve
the reference returned by the iterator. It tries to create a new local
BasicBlock value by copying the existing element. LLVM BasicBlock is
noncopyable, so that loop does not compile.

The appropriate read-only traversal is:

  for (const auto &bb : F) {
      // Inspect the existing block without copying it.
  }

Here const auto &bb is equivalent to const llvm::BasicBlock &bb. The & asks
for reference binding, while const makes access through that reference
read-only.

Compare the declarations:

  auto bb          Try to copy the element into a local value.
  auto &bb         Bind to the existing element, preserving its constness.
  const auto &bb   Bind to the existing element through a const reference.

There is a useful detail in FunctionEligibility::check: F is declared as
const llvm::Function &. Iterating it gives const block references. Thus
auto &bb also deduces a const reference here; it cannot remove constness
from the block. Writing const auto &bb states the read-only intention
explicitly and remains clear when reading the loop on its own.

Connect this lesson to the earlier pointer traversal. For a vector whose
elements are block pointers, auto successor copies only a pointer value.
It does not copy the pointed-to BasicBlock. Plain auto is therefore not
always wrong: its suitability depends on the element type and whether a
copy is intended.

Checkpoint: explain why auto bb fails for F, why const auto &bb succeeds,
and why auto successor can succeed for info.successors. Keep the eligibility
checker read-only and write the code yourself.

20. Explain exception-handling pads and personality checks
=========================================================

During function eligibility work, the learner asked why both
F.hasPersonalityFn() and BB.isEHPad() check exception handling, then asked
what the word pad means.

Start with the control-flow picture. Pad comes from landing pad: a
designated place where execution arrives when an exception interrupts
normal control flow. An invoke instruction names two destinations:

  %result = invoke i32 @callee()
      to label %normal
      unwind label %exception

Normal completion continues at normal. An exceptional unwind transfers
control to exception. In the landing-pad model, exception begins with a
landingpad instruction after any PHIs:

  exception:
    %ex = landingpad { ptr, i32 } cleanup
    resume { ptr, i32 } %ex

This is a fragment inside a function with a suitable personality routine,
not a complete standalone IR module. landingpad receives exception
information. cleanup marks participation in cleanup handling; it does not
execute destructors or other cleanup work automatically. Such operations
would be separate instructions. resume continues unwinding.

Teach the broader term next: an EH pad is an exception-handling entry
instruction. LLVM recognizes landingpad, catchpad, cleanuppad, and
catchswitch as EH-pad instructions.

Distinguish the two queries by scope:

  F.hasPersonalityFn()
    Function-level: does this function specify a personality routine?
    That routine defines language-specific exception-handling behavior.

  BB.isEHPad()
    Block-level: is the block's first non-PHI instruction an EH pad?

Use tests/ir/instruction-kinds.ll as the concrete example. with_invoke has
a personality routine, but only its exception block is an EH pad:

  entry      invoke instruction       isEHPad() is false
  normal     return instruction       isEHPad() is false
  exception  landingpad instruction   isEHPad() is true

An invoke initiates a call with an exceptional destination; it is not
itself an EH pad. A pad marks where exception-handling control arrives.

Clarify the overlap in the eligibility checker instead of claiming both
checks are independently necessary. A function may retain a personality
routine without having any pad blocks. Conversely, valid EH-pad
instructions require a personality routine. Rejecting all personality
functions at the start already rejects functions with valid EH pads.
The later block-level rejection is therefore redundant for those inputs,
though it states the unsupported block kind explicitly.

Checkpoint: identify which block receives the unwind edge, explain why
the block containing invoke is not the pad, and explain why early
personality rejection prevents reaching the pad check in this fixture.

Reference: https://llvm.org/docs/ExceptionHandling.html

21. Relate intrinsics to instructions during coroutine checks
===========================================================

The learner asked how an intrinsic relates to an instruction after adding
coroutine detection to the eligibility checker.

Start with three IR instruction fragments:

  %a = add i32 %x, 1
  %b = call i32 @my_function(i32 %a)
  %c = call double @llvm.sqrt.f64(double %value)

All three lines are instructions. The first is an add operation. The second
calls an ordinary function. The third calls an intrinsic: a special function
with semantics defined by LLVM itself.

An intrinsic is commonly expressed as a function call rather than a new
instruction opcode. Its name has the reserved llvm. prefix. For example,
declare the square-root intrinsic without supplying a function body:

  declare double @llvm.sqrt.f64(double)

LLVM knows the intrinsic's behavior and lowers it to appropriate target
code. It need not remain a machine-level function call. Distinguish the
function declaration from the call instruction: the declaration is a
Function, while the call inside a basic block is an Instruction.

Next connect the concepts to the C++ class hierarchy:

  Instruction
    CallBase
      CallInst
        IntrinsicInst

Each line is derived from the class above it. Every IntrinsicInst is both
a CallInst and an Instruction. Not every Instruction is a call, and not
every call invokes an intrinsic. LLVM permits certain intrinsic uses through
invoke as well; IntrinsicInst specifically represents intrinsic calls with
the call opcode.

Now explain the eligibility check:

  llvm::dyn_cast<llvm::IntrinsicInst>(&I)

This checks whether I is an intrinsic call instruction. Success yields a
pointer that exposes intrinsic-specific information; failure yields nullptr.
For a successful cast, inspect getCalledFunction()->getName(). A name
starting with llvm.coro. identifies the coroutine family.

Use both checks in the initial coroutine policy: reject a function marked
isPresplitCoroutine(), and inspect instructions for coroutine intrinsic
calls. The latter policy is conservative: it also rejects ordinary functions
that manipulate coroutine handles through these intrinsics. Only accept a
function after all function, block, terminator, and instruction checks pass.

Connect this back to the statistics exercise. An intrinsic call still has
opcode Call, so the existing Call case counts it. The counter currently
means all call instructions, including intrinsic calls, rather than only
calls to user-defined functions.

Checkpoint: classify add, an ordinary function call, and an intrinsic call.
Explain why the coroutine name check is needed after dyn_cast succeeds:
recognizing an intrinsic alone does not establish that it is a coroutine
operation; llvm.sqrt.f64 is an intrinsic too.

Reference: https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#intrinsic-functions

22. Integrate eligibility decisions into the flattening stub
==========================================================

After implementing the helper, first replace its temporary final rejection
with return {true, ""};. Keep that success return after all checks and outside
both traversal loops. Correct diagnostic spelling so rejection messages are
clear and stable.

In lib/FlatteningPass.cpp, include minishield/FunctionEligibility.h. After
the existing declaration check, request a decision:

  const auto eligibility = FunctionEligibility::check(F);

This is a plain helper call returning a small result value. It does not
require analysis-manager registration. If eligible is false, print the
function name and rejection reason, then immediately return
PreservedAnalyses::all(). If eligible is true, print an eligible diagnostic.
Keep the final PreservedAnalyses::all(): the stub still changes nothing.

Checkpoint: explain why rejecting before transformation is essential, and
why eligible means passing the current feature checks rather than already
being transformed or proven safe under every possible future algorithm.

Rebuild and run minishield-flatten on tests/ir/phi.ll. merge should be
eligible. Then run tests/ir/instruction-kinds.ll:

  with_invoke             Rejected for its exception-handling personality.
  with_indirectbr         Rejected for the unsupported indirectbr terminator.
  without_optimization    Skipped because FlatteningPass is optional and the
                          function has optnone.

The first two decisions were observed in the built plugin, as was merge's
eligible diagnostic. The optional pass differs from RequiredPassInfoMixin
on StatsPass, which permits inspection of optnone functions. A skipped
function has not received an eligibility decision from FlatteningPass.


23. Isolate rejection checks with new fixtures
=============================================

Next teaching task: write fixtures yourself for coroutine intrinsics and
address-taken blocks. Do not rely only on the existing EH and indirectbr
examples: an earlier rejection can mask the check being tested.

Create tests/ir/coroutine.ll:

  declare ptr @llvm.coro.noop()

  define ptr @coroutine_handle() {
  entry:
    %handle = call ptr @llvm.coro.noop()
    ret ptr %handle
  }

Its return is supported, and it has no personality routine. The expected
rejection is specifically the coroutine-intrinsic check. This is a test of
the conservative rule rejecting coroutine operations, including handle
manipulation; it does not claim this function is itself a presplit coroutine.

Create tests/ir/address_taken.ll:

  define ptr @address_taken() {
  entry:
    br label %target
  target:
    ret ptr blockaddress(@address_taken, %target)
  }

Both terminators are supported, but target has its address used as a value.
Expect rejection for the address-taken block. This tests the block property
independently of the unsupported indirectbr terminator in the other fixture.

After building, run each file through:

  opt -load-pass-plugin="$PWD/build/libMiniShield.so" \
    -passes='function(minishield-flatten),verify' \
    -disable-output tests/ir/coroutine.ll

Repeat using tests/ir/address_taken.ll. Confirm the function name and the
intended rejection reason, rather than treating any rejection as sufficient.


24. Check that rejected functions remain unchanged
=================================================

The rejection contract requires leaving the function untouched. Verification
alone only checks IR validity; valid IR can still be different from input.

For the coroutine fixture, generate comparable canonical IR on both paths:

  opt -passes=verify \
    -S tests/ir/coroutine.ll -o build/before.ll

  opt -load-pass-plugin="$PWD/build/libMiniShield.so" \
    -passes='function(minishield-flatten),verify' \
    -S tests/ir/coroutine.ll -o build/after.ll

  diff -u build/before.ll build/after.ll

Expect a rejection diagnostic and an empty diff. Repeat for address_taken.ll
and the existing negative cases. LLVM prints both outputs, so differences
between handwritten input formatting and LLVM's formatting do not interfere.

The learner wrote both fixtures. The follow-up review ran both through the
plugin: coroutine_handle was rejected for its coroutine intrinsic, and
address_taken was rejected for its address-taken block. Both canonical IR
comparisons were unchanged. The steps above remain the repeatable exercises
for reviewing this rejection contract.

The next negative test should isolate isPresplitCoroutine() separately from
intrinsic detection. After completing the remaining Day 1 checks, review
the roadmap before starting any flattening transformations.

Only then extend the data with terminator information and study dominators
and loop detection. Function eligibility and control-flow flattening come
later in the roadmap. Do not mark them complete based on a statistics table
or a successful verifier run.
